-- title:
--   Every point lies within half the diameter of a scaled lattice cell
-- statement:
--   Let $s > 0$ be a real scale factor and let $x \in \mathbb{R}^d$ be arbitrary. Then there is a lattice point $w \in \mathbb{Z}^d$ with
--
--   $$\lVert x - s\,w \rVert \;\le\; \frac{s}{2}\sqrt{d}.$$
--
--   That is, the rescaled lattice $s\mathbb{Z}^d$ has covering radius at most $\tfrac{s}{2}\sqrt{d}$: every point of $\mathbb{R}^d$ is within that Euclidean distance of some scaled lattice point, the bound being half the diameter of a cell of side $s$.
--
--   The estimate is the routine device for selecting a *centre* near a given point: any construction organized around the vertices of the lattice $s\mathbb{Z}^d$ can be applied at a vertex chosen this close to a prescribed point, at the cost of an error controlled by $s\sqrt{d}$.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The lattice is `lattice d`, defined coordinatewise as the points all of whose coordinates are integers, and the scaling $s \bullet w$ is scalar multiplication in `EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d)`; the norm is the Euclidean one, which is why the bound carries $\sqrt d$ rather than being coordinatewise.
-- source:
